[center]
[color=#ffff00][size=6][b]Translations: Guide[/b][/size][/color][/center]
[line]
SLM reads its interface text from JSON files. Adding a language takes one file, and needs no coding, no compiling, and no mod update.

Two parts: picking a language, and making one.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Picking a language[/b][/size][/color]
[b]Settings[/b] tab, then the [b]Language[/b] dropdown.

[list]
[*][b]Follow the game[/b] (the default) uses whatever language Cyberpunk is set to.
[*]Picking any other language overrides that. SLM stays in it even if you change the game.
[*]Right-click the dropdown to put it back to [b]Follow the game[/b].
[/list]

Only languages you have a file for appear in the list. Anything not yet translated shows in English, one string at a time, so a half-finished translation is still usable.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Non-Latin alphabets need a CET setting[/b][/size][/color]
CET draws SLM's window, and CET loads [b]one[/b] extra alphabet, chosen in its own config. SLM cannot set it, and it does not follow your game's language.

German, French, Spanish, Italian and Portuguese need nothing.

[b]Cyrillic, Polish, Czech, Turkish, Vietnamese, Thai, Chinese, Japanese and Korean[/b] need this in [font=Courier New]bin/x64/plugins/cyber_engine_tweaks/config.json[/font]:

[code]"font": {
    "language": "Cyrillic"
}[/code]

Valid values: [font=Courier New]ChineseFull[/font], [font=Courier New]ChineseSimplifiedCommon[/font], [font=Courier New]Japanese[/font], [font=Courier New]Korean[/font], [font=Courier New]Cyrillic[/font], [font=Courier New]Thai[/font], [font=Courier New]Vietnamese[/font]

Without it those characters draw as blank boxes. CET takes its default from your Windows system language, so it is often already correct.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Making a translation[/b][/size][/color]
Everything lives in:

[code]bin/x64/plugins/cyber_engine_tweaks/mods/SimpleLocationManager/lang/[/code]

[list=1]
[*][b]Copy[/b] [font=Courier New]en-us.json[/font] to [font=Courier New]<code>.json[/font], using the game's own language code - [font=Courier New]de-de[/font], [font=Courier New]fr-fr[/font], [font=Courier New]es-es[/font], [font=Courier New]it-it[/font], [font=Courier New]pl-pl[/font], [font=Courier New]pt-br[/font], [font=Courier New]ru-ru[/font], [font=Courier New]zh-cn[/font], [font=Courier New]ja-jp[/font], [font=Courier New]ko-kr[/font] and so on.
[*][b]Set[/b] [font=Courier New]"@name"[/font] to your language's name written in that language - [font=Courier New]Deutsch[/font], not [font=Courier New]German[/font]. The dropdown shows this value.
[*][b]Translate the values.[/b] Leave the keys alone.
[*][b]Save it in that folder[/b] and restart the game.
[/list]

SLM scans the folder at startup, so your language appears on its own. No slot has to exist for it beforehand.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]The rules[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*][b]Translate values, never keys.[/b] [font=Courier New]"edit.save": "Speichern"[/font] is right. Renaming the key leaves a string nothing looks up, and you will see [font=Courier New]edit.save[/font] on screen.
[*][b]Keep every[/b] [font=Courier New]%s[/font] [b]and[/b] [font=Courier New]%d[/font][b].[/b] They are slots SLM fills at runtime with a name, a count or a district. [font=Courier New]%s[/font] takes text, [font=Courier New]%d[/font] takes a number.
[*][b]Move the slots where your language needs them.[/b] Number them to reorder: [font=Courier New]%1$[/font] is the first value SLM passes, [font=Courier New]%2$[/font] the second. So [font=Courier New]"Export Category: %s (%d)"[/font] can become [font=Courier New]"Kategorie exportieren: (%2$d) %1$s"[/font]. Number every slot in a string or none of them.
[*][b]Do not add or remove slots.[/b] A slot SLM has no value for renders as a literal [font=Courier New]%s[/font].
[*][b]Keep[/b] [font=Courier New]\n[/font] [b]where it appears.[/b] It is a line break inside a tooltip.
[/list]

A partial translation works. Delete the keys you have not done and each one falls back to English on its own.

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]What is not in this file[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*][b]District names[/b] come from the game, which already names every district in each of its nineteen languages. They follow the game's language even when SLM is pinned to another, and they match the world map.
[*][b]The categories SLM ships with[/b] are in this file, under [font=Courier New]category.[/font] - a category you made yourself is your own words and stays as you named it.
[*][b]Icon names[/b] in the icon picker come from the icon library.
[*][b]Log levels[/b] (Off, Error, Warn, Info, Debug) are diagnostic terms and stay English.
[/list]

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Checks before sharing[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*]The file is [b]valid JSON[/b]. One trailing comma or missing quote and SLM logs a warning and falls back to English for the whole file.
[*]The file is saved as [b]UTF-8[/b].
[*]Every [font=Courier New]%s[/font] and [font=Courier New]%d[/font] in a value also appears in the English value for that key.
[*]Open the mod and walk the tabs. A string in English is a key you have not done yet; a string showing as [font=Courier New]edit.save[/font] is a key that got renamed.
[/list]

[line]
[color=#ffff00][size=5][b]Reference, and sharing your work[/b][/size][/color]
The full reference ships with the mod, beside the language files:

[code]bin/x64/plugins/cyber_engine_tweaks/mods/SimpleLocationManager/lang/TRANSLATING.md[/code]

It is on GitHub too, with the current [font=Courier New]en-us.json[/font] so you can see the newest strings:

[list]
[*][url=https://github.com/spuddeh/simple-location-manager/blob/main/SimpleLocationManager/lang/TRANSLATING.md][b]TRANSLATING.md[/b][/url]
[*][url=https://github.com/spuddeh/simple-location-manager/blob/main/SimpleLocationManager/lang/en-us.json][b]en-us.json[/b][/url] (the English strings)
[*][url=https://github.com/spuddeh/simple-location-manager][b]The repository[/b][/url]
[/list]

To get a translation bundled with the mod:

[list]
[*][b]Open a pull request[/b] on GitHub, or
[*][b]Post it in the comments[/b] here, or
[*][b]Upload it as your own mod[/b] - it is one file dropped in a folder, so it needs no permission from me. Link it in the comments and I will point people at it.
[/list]

When SLM adds new strings your file keeps working. The new keys show in English until someone fills them in.
